{:edb/db-before-t 2813 :edb/db-after-t 2814 :edb/tx-data [[13194139536126 50 #inst "2026-10-09T15:48:03.522Z" 13194139536126 true] [17592186119436 1145 "The point $P(1,1,1)$ is at the tip of $\\mathbf r(t)$ for some specific value $t_0$ of the parameter $t.$ At this point,  the vector $\\mathbf r'(t_0)$ is tangent to the curve.\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to $P.$ We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\,i+\\,j+\\,k \\\\ t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j+{t}^{3}\\,k & =i+\\,j+\\,k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf i, \\mathbf j,$ and $\\mathbf k$ yields the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}t=1 \\\\ {t}^{2}=1 \\\\ {t}^{3}=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $t=1.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(1,1,1)$ is at the tip of the vector $\\mathbf r(1).$  \n\nNow, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})\\,k \\\\  & =i+2t\\,j+3{t}^{2}\\,k \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =i+2(1)\\,j+3(1)^{2}\\,k \\\\  & =i+2\\,j+3\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to the given curve at at the point $P(1,1,1)$ is $\\,\\mathbf i+2\\,\\mathbf j+3\\,\\mathbf k.$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/assets/bae639af7c4267014af4e558f9a21b042470a3f7d46fa7a31ac0162c7b08ef08.png)" 13194139536126 false] [17592186119436 1145 "The point $P(1,1,1)$ is at the tip of $\\mathbf r(t)$ for some specific value $t_0$ of the parameter $t.$ At this point,  the vector $\\mathbf r'(t_0)$ is tangent to the curve.\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to $P.$ We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\,i+\\,j+\\,k \\\\ t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j+{t}^{3}\\,k & =i+\\,j+\\,k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf i, \\mathbf j,$ and $\\mathbf k$ yields the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}t=1 \\\\ {t}^{2}=1 \\\\ {t}^{3}=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $t=1.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(1,1,1)$ is at the tip of the vector $\\mathbf r(1).$  \n\nNow, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})\\,k \\\\  & =i+2t\\,j+3{t}^{2}\\,k \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =i+2(1)\\,j+3(1)^{2}\\,k \\\\  & =i+2\\,j+3\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to the given curve at at the point $P(1,1,1)$ is $\\,\\mathbf i+2\\,\\mathbf j+3\\,\\mathbf k.$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/assets/bae639af7c4267014af4e558f9a21b042470a3f7d46fa7a31ac0162c7b08ef08.png)" 13194139536126 true] [17592186119437 1145 "Let  $P$ be the point at the tip of the vector $\\mathbf r\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right ).$  At this point,  the vector $\\mathbf r'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right )$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf R(u)=\\mathbf r\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right )+u \\, \\mathbf r'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right ), \\qquad u \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we find $\\mathbf r\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,4{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}⟩=⟨0,1,{\\pi }^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow,  we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,8t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,8(\\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,4\\pi ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the given curve at the point $P$ where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(u)=⟨0,1,{\\pi }^{2}⟩+u⟨-1,0,4\\pi ⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/assets/e24b811fb8d9e43bd0682e3686f8b2ef1198e36016978ca305a7df4dffe21889.png)" 13194139536126 false] [17592186119437 1145 "Let  $P$ be the point at the tip of the vector $\\mathbf r\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right ).$  At this point,  the vector $\\mathbf r'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right )$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf R(u)=\\mathbf r\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right )+u \\, \\mathbf r'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right ), \\qquad u \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we find $\\mathbf r\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,4{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}⟩=⟨0,1,{\\pi }^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow,  we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,8t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,8(\\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,4\\pi ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the given curve at the point $P$ where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(u)=⟨0,1,{\\pi }^{2}⟩+u⟨-1,0,4\\pi ⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/assets/e24b811fb8d9e43bd0682e3686f8b2ef1198e36016978ca305a7df4dffe21889.png)" 13194139536126 true] [17592186119438 1145 "First, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-t)\\,j \\\\  & =(2t+1)\\,i+(2t-1)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j$ are parallel for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that $\\mathbf r'(t)=c(3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j).$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2t+1)\\,i+(2t-1)\\,j=c(3\\,i+\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\mathbf i$ and $\\mathbf j$ gives the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2t+1=3c \\\\ 2t-1=c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $c=2t-1.$ Substituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2t+1 & =3c \\\\ 2t+1 & =3(2t-1) \\\\ 2t+1 & =6t-3 \\\\ 6t-2t & =1+3 \\\\ 4t & =4 \\\\ t & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\mathbf r'$ and $3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j$ are parallel only at the point corresponding to $t=1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =({1}^{2}+1)\\,i+({1}^{2}-1)\\,j=2\\,i,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j$ are parallel at the point $(2,0).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/assets/0f723e89b1c91367d6488e034f8b627b3c406aed6d8d1ffc244d6f014021e04e.png)" 13194139536126 false] [17592186119438 1145 "First, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-t)\\,j \\\\  & =(2t+1)\\,i+(2t-1)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j$ are parallel for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that $\\mathbf r'(t)=c(3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j).$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2t+1)\\,i+(2t-1)\\,j=c(3\\,i+\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\mathbf i$ and $\\mathbf j$ gives the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2t+1=3c \\\\ 2t-1=c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $c=2t-1.$ Substituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2t+1 & =3c \\\\ 2t+1 & =3(2t-1) \\\\ 2t+1 & =6t-3 \\\\ 6t-2t & =1+3 \\\\ 4t & =4 \\\\ t & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\mathbf r'$ and $3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j$ are parallel only at the point corresponding to $t=1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =({1}^{2}+1)\\,i+({1}^{2}-1)\\,j=2\\,i,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j$ are parallel at the point $(2,0).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/assets/0f723e89b1c91367d6488e034f8b627b3c406aed6d8d1ffc244d6f014021e04e.png)" 13194139536126 true] [17592186119864 1020 #edb/ref 17592186384787 13194139536126 true] [17592186119864 1020 #edb/ref 17592186384794 13194139536126 true] [17592186119864 1020 #edb/ref 17592186384857 13194139536126 true] [17592186119865 1020 #edb/ref 17592186384801 13194139536126 true] [17592186119865 1020 #edb/ref 17592186384843 13194139536126 true] [17592186119866 1020 #edb/ref 17592186384829 13194139536126 true] [17592186119866 1020 #edb/ref 17592186384836 13194139536126 true] [17592186119866 1020 #edb/ref 17592186384850 13194139536126 true] [17592186119867 1020 #edb/ref 17592186384808 13194139536126 true] [17592186119867 1020 #edb/ref 17592186384815 13194139536126 true] [17592186119867 1020 #edb/ref 17592186384822 13194139536126 true] [17592186119867 1020 #edb/ref 17592186384864 13194139536126 true] [17592186384787 1017 #edb/ref 17592186384788 13194139536126 true] [17592186384787 1089 "q-126740" 13194139536126 true] [17592186384787 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536126 true] [17592186384787 1100 "Find a tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=2\\sqrt{3-t}\\,i+(1-2t)\\,j+(t+3)\\,k$ at the point $P(2,-3,5).$" 13194139536126 true] [17592186384787 1113 #uuid "8009a1f3-562e-594d-a545-8fc2a763c667" 13194139536126 true] [17592186384787 1145 "The point $P(2,-3,5)$ is at the tip of $r(t)$ for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t.$ At this point, the vector ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})$ is tangent to the curve.\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to $P.$ We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =2\\,i-3\\,j+5\\,k \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3-t}\\,i+(1-2t)\\,j+(t+3)\\,k & =2\\,i-3\\,j+5\\,k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand equating the coefficients of $i,j,$ and $k$ yields the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3-t}=2 \\\\ 1-2t=-3 \\\\ t+3=5\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $t=2.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(2,-3,5)$ is at the tip of the vector $r(2).$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{3-t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-2t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+3)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3-t}}\\,i-2\\,j+k \\\\ {r}^{′}(2) & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3-2}}\\,i-2\\,j+k \\\\  & =-\\,i-2\\,j+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to the given curve at the point $P(2,-3,5)$ is $-\\,i-2\\,j+k.$" 13194139536126 true] [17592186384788 1000 #edb/ref 17592186384789 13194139536126 true] [17592186384788 1000 #edb/ref 17592186384790 13194139536126 true] [17592186384788 1000 #edb/ref 17592186384791 13194139536126 true] [17592186384788 1000 #edb/ref 17592186384792 13194139536126 true] [17592186384788 1000 #edb/ref 17592186384793 13194139536126 true] [17592186384788 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536126 true] [17592186384788 1041 "selection" 13194139536126 true] [17592186384788 1104 #edb/ref 17592186384791 13194139536126 true] [17592186384788 1110 #uuid "4a53b87b-f96e-5fa7-96f0-b4c84d3a262d" 13194139536126 true] [17592186384789 1034 "-\\,i+j+3\\,k" 13194139536126 true] [17592186384789 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536126 true] [17592186384789 1111 #uuid "0ed776e7-5556-5e07-9003-cc39c86c5882" 13194139536126 true] [17592186384790 1034 "2\\,i-2\\,j+k" 13194139536126 true] [17592186384790 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536126 true] [17592186384790 1111 #uuid "be93162f-bfae-5e1c-a834-119877a93ab7" 13194139536126 true] [17592186384791 1034 "-\\,i-2\\,j+k" 13194139536126 true] [17592186384791 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536126 true] [17592186384791 1111 #uuid "20f1048b-362a-5a39-8ad5-464b7454e370" 13194139536126 true] [17592186384792 1034 "-2\\,i-3\\,j+k" 13194139536126 true] [17592186384792 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536126 true] [17592186384792 1111 #uuid "4fc04868-9c6c-5c3c-b5d1-efa9696ef37b" 13194139536126 true] [17592186384793 1034 "\\,i+j-2\\,k" 13194139536126 true] [17592186384793 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536126 true] [17592186384793 1111 #uuid "c9002579-9caa-5784-898d-3127cede01ed" 13194139536126 true] [17592186384794 1017 #edb/ref 17592186384795 13194139536126 true] [17592186384794 1089 "q-126741" 13194139536126 true] [17592186384794 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536126 true] [17592186384794 1100 "Find a vector that's tangent to the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{3}-1,{t}^{2}+1⟩$ at the point where $t=-2.$" 13194139536126 true] [17592186384794 1113 #uuid "844f2cf4-dad9-5ec2-9ea0-6093c575cda0" 13194139536126 true] [17592186384794 1145 "At the point where $t=-2,$ the vector ${r}^{′}(-2)$ is tangent to the curve.\n\nLet's calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=-2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{